=== layout: two-panels

# Chromosome nomenclature

@left

- [*A*]{underline=true}: one set of autosomes.
- [*AA, AAA, AAAA*]{underline=true}: two, three, or four autosome sets.
- A typical XX fruit fly has 8 chromosomes: 2, 2, 3, 3, 4, 4, X, X.
- XX:AAA has 11 chromosomes: three copies each of chromosomes 2, 3, and 4, plus two X chromosomes.

@right

![Chromosome nomenclature](assets/lect06e-x_linked_genes_2/autosome_set_nomenclature.svg)

@notes

Source: lect06e-x_linked_genes_2.odp, slide 17. Replacement reason: dense text requires post-conversion polish; normalized distributed pictorial annotations into native source-order content | Imported content for reconstruction: | Original layout: two-panels | # Note on Nomenclature | Original slot: left | - wildtype drosophila females are 8,XX | - 8 chromosomes total and 2 X chromosomes | - 2,2,3,3,4,4,X,X | - XX\:AAA | - this drosophila would be 11,XX | - 11 chromosomes total and 2 X chromosomes | - 2,2,2,3,3,3,4,4,4,X,X | - AAA means that the organism is triploid, 3n, three copies of every chromosome | - so in drosophila\: | - 3 copies of chromosome 2 | - 3 copies of chromosome 3 | - 3 copies of chromosome 4 | - but they would only have two X chromosomes | Original slot: right | ![Slide image 5](assets/lect06e-x_linked_genes_2/image_005.png) | - A | - AA | - AAA | - `AAAA` | Imported reference content: | Original layout: big-image | Original slot: replaceme | # Note on Nomenclature | Original slot: image | ![Slide image 5](assets/lect06e-x_linked_genes_2/image_005.png) | Original slot: caption | Restore positioned labels and relationships from source slide 17.

